<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José Félix Uriburu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José Félix Uriburu was an Argentine general who overthrew President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hipolito-yrigoyen">Hipólito Yrigoyen</a> on 6 September 1930 and ruled as de facto president until February 1932. He hoped to replace the democratic system with a corporatist order, but lost the support of the conservatives, who restored elections under the fraudulent conditions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/infamous-decade">Infamous Decade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-felix-uriburu.jpg" alt="José Félix Uriburu" width="320" height="468" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edeiaid · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jose_Uriburu.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>José Félix Benito Uriburu was born on 20 July 1868 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, the nephew of President José Evaristo Uriburu. He entered the Colegio Militar de la Nación as a cadet on 17 March 1885 and, as a sublieutenant, was one of the 33 officers who took part in organising the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolution-of-the-park">Revolution of the Park</a> in 1890. In 1905 he supported President Manuel Quintana in suppressing the Radical Revolution. He directed the Superior School of War from 1907, trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> for three years and was military attaché to Germany and the United Kingdom in 1913. Elected to the National Congress in 1914, he became a Division General in 1921, and Yrigoyen forced him to retire in 1926 on reaching retirement age.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The world economic crisis of 1929 hit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> hard, since 80 per cent of its revenue came from foreign trade, and brought falling wages and rising unemployment. Catholic nationalists, drawn to the corporatist ideas of the Church's social teaching and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini</a>'s Italy, published La Nueva República against the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hipolito-yrigoyen">Hipólito Yrigoyen</a>, who was criticised for federal interventions and for ties to the killing of opposition leaders. On 6 September 1930 Uriburu overthrew Yrigoyen. The poet Leopoldo Lugones wrote the proclamation, whose first version Colonel José María Sarobe and General Agustín P. Justo accused of being fascist.</p><p>On 10 September the Supreme Court recognised Uriburu as president, giving rise to the de facto government doctrine. He dissolved the National Congress, declared a state of siege and attempted to establish a neo-corporatist government. He banned political parties, suspended elections and the 1853 Constitution, and expressed opposition to universal suffrage. His regime used torture against political opponents, executed anarchist militants including Severino Di Giovanni, and took over the universities. In early 1931 he called elections in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> but annulled them after the Radical Civic Union won. In November 1931 he called elections after prohibiting UCR candidates, and General Justo was elected president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After handing power to Justo he left the country for health reasons and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> two months later after surgery for stomach cancer. His body was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and buried in Recoleta Cemetery. His was the first of a series of coups in Argentina, followed in 1943, 1955, 1962, 1966 and 1976. Busts and monuments raised in his honour have been removed in the 21st century. San Carlos de Bolívar's council voted in 2012 to remove its bust, and the bust in Olavarría was taken down in January 2019.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “José Félix Uriburu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salta">Salta</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-24.79,-65.41&amp;year=1909" aria-label="Salta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2814 3076 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of José Félix Uriburu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3264 3346h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3264 3346h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-24.79,-65.41&amp;year=1909">What was here in 1909? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
